{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd0b8236e42d156a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "50300 paper_text.txt\nAbstract Download PDF Abstract. 1 Introduction 2 Related Work 3 Dataset and Tasks 3.1 Task Overview 3.2 Dataset and Recording Setup 3.3 Baseline Pipeline 3.4 Evaluation Metrics Speaker-dependent WER Conversation Clustering F1. Joint ASR-Clustering Error. 4 Taxonomy and Design Space 4.1 Target-Speaker Transcription Pipelines Segmentation: explicit ASD versus long-context recognition. Target-speaker extraction : explicit extraction versus implicit conditioning. Recognition backbones: 4.2 Conversation Clustering Strategies Temporal clustering from speech activity: Semantic clustering with large language models: Visual interaction cues and multimodal fusion: 4.3 Cross-Cutting Design Patterns 4.4 Training Data and Supervision 5 Results 6 Conclusion Acknowledgements References License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 arXiv:2608.08510v1 [cs.CL] 09 Aug 2026 \\setcctype by From Speech to Interaction: Analyzing Multimodal Systems in Cocktail-Party Scenarios Thai-Binh Nguyen Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany thai-binh.nguyen@kit.edu , Zhaolin Li Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany zhaolin.li@kit.edu , Jan Niehues Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany jan.niehues@kit.edu and Alexander Waibel Carnegie Mellon University Pittsburgh USA zhaolin.li@kit.edu (2026) Abstract. Humans have the remarkable ability to engage in spontaneous informal conversations and selectively attend to individual speakers while filtering out competing speech from nearby conversations. This “cocktail party” scenario still presents severe challenges to speech recognition systems. The CHiME-9 MCoRec task provides a testbed where systems must recognize groups of speakers and transcribe each of their conversations from audio-visual input. In this work, we analyze a diverse set of systems, representing different design directions for addressing the cocktail-party scenario, where the best system achieves up to 57% relative error reduction. We identify three main strategies: (1)\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa2d2247-9744-476c-9dbc-83a3513d5460", "uuid": "69038a3b-b51b-430e-b394-7ef52b8689c4", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T23:11:04.808Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "50300 paper_text.txt\nAbstract Download PDF Abstract. 1 Introduction 2 Related Work 3 Dataset and Tasks 3.1 Task Overview 3.2 Dataset and Recording Setup 3.3 Baseline Pipeline 3.4 Evaluation Metrics Speaker-dependent WER Conversation Clustering F1. Joint ASR-Clustering Error. 4 Taxonomy and Design Space 4.1 Target-Speaker Transcription Pipelines Segmentation: explicit ASD versus long-context recognition. Target-speaker extraction : explicit extraction versus implicit conditioning. Recognition backbones: 4.2 Conversation Clustering Strategies Temporal clustering from speech activity: Semantic clustering with large language models: Visual interaction cues and multimodal fusion: 4.3 Cross-Cutting Design Patterns 4.4 Training Data and Supervision 5 Results 6 Conclusion Acknowledgements References License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 arXiv:2608.08510v1 [cs.CL] 09 Aug 2026 \\setcctype by From Speech to Interaction: Analyzing Multimodal Systems in Cocktail-Party Scenarios Thai-Binh Nguyen Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany thai-binh.nguyen@kit.edu , Zhaolin Li Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany zhaolin.li@kit.edu , Jan Niehues Karlsruhe Institute of Technology Karlsruhe Germany jan.niehues@kit.edu and Alexander Waibel Carnegie Mellon University Pittsburgh USA zhaolin.li@kit.edu (2026) Abstract. Humans have the remarkable ability to engage in spontaneous informal conversations and selectively attend to individual speakers while filtering out competing speech from nearby conversations. This “cocktail party” scenario still presents severe challenges to speech recognition systems. The CHiME-9 MCoRec task provides a testbed where systems must recognize groups of speakers and transcribe each of their conversations from audio-visual input. In this work, we analyze a diverse set of systems, representing different design directions for addressing the cocktail-party scenario, where the best system achieves up to 57% relative error reduction. We identify three main strategies: (1)", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
